labels such as \code{"week_4"} is refused rather than coerced to 1, 2, 3,
which would assert an equal spacing the study may not have.

\code{k} is the size of the spline basis: how much shape the curve is allowed
to have. \code{mgcv} defaults to 10, which fails below ten distinct time
points, so the default here is \code{min(n_distinct_times - 1, 10)}, read from
the design. The penalty, not \code{k}, decides how wiggly the fitted curve
actually is, which is what the reported \code{edf} measures: \code{edf} near 1 means
the fit was flattened to a line.

\code{subject_effect} says how repeated measures from one subject are handled.
Treating them as independent would be pseudo-replication and would make
every taxon look significant.

\itemize{
\item \code{"intercept"} — \code{s(subject, bs = "re")}. Subjects differ in
level but share a shape. The usual case in microbiome data, and the
default.
\item \code{"slope"} — adds \code{s(subject, time, bs = "re")}. Subjects also
drift apart over time, so between-subject spread grows. Needs about
four time points per subject.
\item \code{"smooth"} — \code{s(time, subject, bs = "fs", m = 1)}. Each subject
gets its own curve, penalised toward the mean. Needs about six time
points per subject, and in \code{\link[=mc_compare]{mc_compare()}} it can absorb the group
difference being tested; a warning is raised when that applies.
\item \code{"none"} — nothing added. For one sample per subject.
}

These structures are the ones described for \code{mgcv} by Wood (2017) and, for
group-varying smooths, by Pedersen et al. (2019).
